# UI & UX Best Practices

When building or updating UI, match the existing app’s visual language and follow current UX conventions. Favor a modern, sleek, scannable interface that is easy to understand and easy to use.

## Design principles

- **Clarity first**: One primary action per view. Use clear headings, short helper text, and obvious next steps.
- **Consistency**: Reuse existing layout patterns, spacing, typography, and component variants from the codebase before inventing new ones.
- **Progressive disclosure**: Show essentials first; use `Dialog`, `Sheet`, `Collapsible`, or tabs for secondary detail.
- **Feedback**: Always handle loading, empty, error, and success states with shadcn primitives (`Skeleton`, `Alert`, `Toast`/`Sonner`, `Badge`).
- **Accessibility**: Use semantic structure, visible labels, keyboard-friendly controls, and sufficient contrast. Prefer shadcn components over custom interactive elements.

## shadcn-first implementation

- Compose UI from shadcn components and project wrappers. Install missing components via the shadcn CLI before building custom equivalents.
- Prefer established patterns from [shadcn docs](https://ui.shadcn.com/docs/components) and [blocks](https://ui.shadcn.com/blocks): forms, data tables, sidebars, settings pages, command palettes.
- Use Tailwind utility classes and design tokens (`bg-background`, `text-muted-foreground`, `border`, `rounded-lg`, consistent `gap-*` / `p-*`) instead of one-off styles.

## Layout & polish

- Use responsive layouts (`flex`, `grid`, `container`) with comfortable whitespace; avoid cramped or overly dense screens.
- Group related content in `Card` sections with concise titles and descriptions.
- Align actions predictably: primary actions on the right or bottom of forms; destructive actions use `variant="destructive"` with confirmation.
- Use icons sparingly for wayfinding (`lucide-react`), not decoration.

## Forms & data

- Pair every input with a `Label`; show inline validation with `Form` + `FormMessage`.
- Prefer `Select`, `Combobox`, or `RadioGroup` over free-text when choices are known.
- For lists and tables, include search/filter, sort, and empty states where appropriate.

## Examples

```tsx
// ❌ BAD — raw elements, no states, unclear hierarchy
<div>
  <h1>Settings</h1>
  <input placeholder="Name" />
  <button onClick={save}>Save</button>
</div>

// ✅ GOOD — shadcn structure, clear hierarchy, labeled form
<Card>
  <CardHeader>
    <CardTitle>Settings</CardTitle>
    <CardDescription>Update your organization details.</CardDescription>
  </CardHeader>
  <CardContent>
    <Form {...form}>
      <FormField name="name" render={({ field }) => (
        <FormItem>
          <FormLabel>Name</FormLabel>
          <FormControl><Input {...field} /></FormControl>
          <FormMessage />
        </FormItem>
      )} />
    </Form>
  </CardContent>
  <CardFooter className="justify-end">
    <Button onClick={form.handleSubmit(onSubmit)} disabled={isPending}>
      {isPending ? "Saving…" : "Save changes"}
    </Button>
  </CardFooter>
</Card>
```
